**How to Print the Changelist in Java**

In Java, you can print the changelist of an `AppVersion` object by calling the `printChangelist()` method.

```java
public class AppVersion {
    private int major;
    private int minor;
    private int patch;

    public AppVersion(int major, int minor, int patch) {
        this.major = major;
        this.minor = minor;
        this.patch = patch;
    }

    public void printChangelist() {
        System.out.println("New changelist with changes to " + major + "." + minor + "." + patch);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AppVersion appVersion = new AppVersion(1, 2, 3);
        appVersion.printChangelist(); // Output: New changelist with changes to 1.2.3
    }
}
```

In this example, the `printChangelist()` method is called on an instance of `AppVersion`, which prints out the changelist in the format "New changelist with changes to X.X.X".

Note that you can also catch any exceptions that may be thrown by the `printChangelist()` method using a try-catch block.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AppVersion appVersion = new AppVersion(1, 2, 3);
        try {
            appVersion.printChangelist();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

This code will print out any error message that may be thrown by the `printChangelist()` method.
