**How to Handle DeprecationWarning in Java**

In Java, you can handle `DeprecationWarning` by catching it in a try-catch block.

```java
public class AppVersion {
    private int major;
    private int minor;
    private int patch;

    public AppVersion(int major, int minor, int patch) {
        this.major = major;
        this.minor = minor;
        this.patch = patch;
    }

    @Deprecated
    public void printChangelist() {
        System.out.println("New changelist with changes to " + major + "." + minor + "." + patch);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AppVersion appVersion = new AppVersion(1, 2, 3);

        try {
            // This will throw a DeprecationWarning
            @SuppressWarnings("deprecation")
            appVersion.printChangelist();
        } catch (DeprecationWarning e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the `printChangelist()` method is marked as deprecated, which means it will throw a `DeprecationWarning` if called. The warning message is caught and printed to the console.

You can also use the `@Deprecated` annotation to mark methods or classes as deprecated, and then handle the warning using a try-catch block.

Note that you can also suppress the warning by adding the `@SuppressWarnings("deprecation")` annotation before calling the deprecated method.
